  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Oxides of metals are generally basic in nature but some are amphoteric in nature.</p><p>Oxides of non-metals are acidic in nature but some of them are neutral.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is acidic in nature it reacts with bases.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Al</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is amphoteric in nature as it reacts with both acids and bases.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>SiO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is acidic in nature.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CaO</mi></math>&nbsp;is a basic oxide and it reacts with acids readily.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
